/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Plus One
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Arrays
Difficulty : Easy

Problem:
Given an array of digits representing a large integer,
increment the integer by one and return the resulting array
of digits.

Approach:
Start from the last digit because adding one affects the
least significant digit first.

If the current digit is less than 9, simply increment it
and return the array because no carry remains.

If the digit is 9, change it to 0 and continue moving toward
the left because the carry must be propagated to the next
digit.

If every digit is 9, all digits become 0 and an additional
leading 1 must be inserted.

Why does this work?

Adding one affects only the suffix of consecutive 9s.

For example:

    [1,2,9,9]
          ↑ ↑
        carry propagates

After processing the 9s:

    [1,3,0,0]

By processing from right to left, we handle the carry exactly
as ordinary decimal addition does.

Algorithm:
1. Start from the last digit.
2. If the digit is less than 9, increment it and return.
3. If the digit is 9, change it to 0 and continue left.
4. If all digits were 9, insert 1 at the beginning.
5. Return the resulting array.

Time Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
In the worst case, every digit is processed once.

Space Complexity : O(1)

Reason:
The operation is performed in-place. Only when all digits are
9 do we insert one additional digit into the result.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
public:
    vector<int> plusOne(vector<int>& digits) {

        // Start from the least significant digit.
        for (int index = digits.size() - 1; index >= 0; index--) {

            // No carry is required after incrementing.
            if (digits[index] < 9) {
                digits[index]++;
                return digits;
            }

            // 9 becomes 0 and carries 1 to the next digit.
            digits[index] = 0;
        }

        // All digits were 9, so add a new leading 1.
        digits.insert(digits.begin(), 1);

        return digits;
    }
};
